    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "ALM\n00000000000000099\nANTILLEAN AIRLINES\nPJ-SNA\nDOUGLAS DC-9-32\nWhen ALM Antillean Airlines acquired as well as in the interior of the new air-\ntheir new DC-9-32 Series aircraft in Jan-\ncraft; warm yellow, sky blue and cool\nuary 1975 they. also introduced a brand\nbreezy white.\nnew livery as illustrated above.\nThe national flag of the Netherlands\nMcDonnell Douglas' Art Director, Dick Antilles is carried on the aircrafts nose.\nCreadick visited the Dutch islands in\nThe flag - a blue and white cross shows\nthe Caribbean and was struck by the\nsix white stars and these six stars are also\ncombination of old 18th century country\nrepeated on the tail of the aircraft - they\nhouses, most of them painted orange/\nyellow\nsymbolise the six islands of the Nether-\nand the expansive blue sky\nlands Antilles. Aruba, Bonaire, Curacao,\nadorned by stately sailing woolly white\nSaba, St. Maarten,\nSt. Eustatius. The\ncolours. Thus the glorious colours of the\nDC-9-32 aircraft are named after the first\nCaribbean were expressed on the outside\nthree islands listed.\nThe new livery has also been applied to\nthe ALM Queen Air and to the Twin\nOtter PJ-WIA previously operated by\nWindward Island Airways of St. Maarten,\nwhich company is also owned by the\nGovernment of the Netherland Antilles\nAn\nof which the ALM President, Mr. C.O.\nYrausquin is also president.\n(Photograph and information kindly sup-\nplied by A.L.M.)\nANTILLEAN AIRLINES",
